387fefcb90d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{1}{2})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505785 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505826 #edb/ref 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{27})}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72480b1b-c0e7-5d75-9725-3eb6bbb0790c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e93dfe2-b7b4-51a0-ae97-f197358d92d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186275347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e0b0b1f-b7bd-5ad1-beb2-38585f99a953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ded